Finite elements from virtual work to an assembled model (@mech-finite-elements: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "An-inspectable-thermoelastic-axial-bar.lingua#r_5ADP35GB2A1A5V37FEFWQB9HVB") { r_7T1F6870T0VKQAGE9QWFC2TA29
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Finite Element Analysis; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), chapters 7–8, beginning with the derivation and demonstration implementation. Work the complete linear-elastic formulation before moving to nonlinear examples.

Approximate displacement inside each element with shape functions and nodal values. Differentiate to obtain strain, apply a constitutive law and enforce virtual work to obtain element equations. Assembly joins contributions at shared nodes. Displacement constraints remove permitted rigid motion and introduce reactions; applied loads belong to a consistent force vector.

For a two-node axial element of length L and constant EA, stiffness is (EA/L)[[1,−1],[−1,1]]. A uniform axial line force p gives consistent nodal forces pL/2 at each end. A uniform prescribed free strain ε₀ contributes equivalent forces (−EAε₀, +EAε₀). Derive these terms from the same weak form and verify their signs with a freely expanding element.

A small algebraic residual only establishes that the discrete equations were solved accurately. Spatial discretization, integration rules, element distortion, boundary conditions and constitutive assumptions can still be wrong. Locking, spurious zero-energy modes and nearly incompressible behavior require appropriate elements and checks.

**Work:** Derive the axial stiffness and force vectors, assemble a two-element problem and solve it by hand. Recover reactions and compare with whole-system equilibrium. The following original program makes this account inspectable before the advanced three-dimensional or nonlinear studies.

